	It's a cool idea, but I'm not sure it'd really work in practice.
Firstly, most of the enemies in the upper dungeon are faceless
non-entities... that rock troll from DL15 may have almost kicked your
butt, but it's indistinguishable from any other random rock troll. That
pretty much leaves just the Quest Nemesis, Croesus, and Medusa.
Shopkeepers if you're in the habit of killing them.

	Secondly, you've beaten these guys once, and you've presumably
gotten more powerful since then. Enemies that were epic battles for a
character who just qualified for his Quest are generally going to be
nothing more than speed bumps for a 20th level character who's gotten to
Gehennom. And some of the Quest Nemeses aren't epic battles even at
14th level. (My last wizard one-shotted the Dark One. He's not
FoD-resistant until he picks up the Eye. I had more trouble with the
vampire bats on Home 1.)

> Not only would it make Gehennom more challenging, it would also
> discourage some of the more dramatic forms of abuse.  Are you really
> going to spend thousands of turns pudding farming, when there's a very
> real chance that doing so means you'll have to face Juiblex and Orcus
> again?  Possibly together?  

	Well, if I were going to bore myself senseless pudding farming
in the first place... yeah. I've always got ten times more wands of
digging than I need, and my SDSM doesn't wear out. Hey, I could farm
Orcus for wands of death!

